questions.answer:
export function renderComment(el, text) {
  el.textContent = text;
  el.style.whiteSpace = "pre-wrap";
}

const dirty = '<img src=x onerror="alert(1)">\nHello Kakade';
renderComment(document.querySelector("#out"), dirty);

Using textContent makes the browser treat the whole string as text: the angle brackets are displayed, the img is never created, and onerror never fires. white-space: pre-wrap preserves the newline without converting it to <br>, so no HTML parsing is needed. If the design truly requires bold links inside comments, sanitize with an allowlist (DOMPurify) instead of switching back to innerHTML on raw input. Never build HTML by concatenating user strings.

